  "live_records": {
    "q-71999": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71999",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}-\\sqrt{x+y}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-2x-2y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x-y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
