    "q-132637": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132637",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y+z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $y+z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y+z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ y+z & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132637-a",
