    "q-36525": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36525",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2x}{2(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
