    "q-72025": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72025",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-72025-a",
