      "problem": "Given that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ is defined only when $xz>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${h}_{x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{y}=-\\frac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cap \\,\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
