    "q-72022": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72022",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy(z-1))?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $z$ is defined at $(3,2,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,1,2)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(1,2,3)$ is defined",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot y(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot x(z-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{xy(z-1)}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{z-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.  \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(3,2,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2\\cdot (1-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. If we substitute $(1,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot (2-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,2) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(1,2,3)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,3) & =\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III  only'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
