    "q-72015": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72015",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-(-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,-1) & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{1}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,-1) & =-\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0).\n$$\n\nwhich is also undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
