    "q-36522": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36522",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(1,-1,0)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{z}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1,1)=\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,1) & =-\\frac{2(0)(1)}{(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1,0)=\\frac{(0)^{2}}{(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'.",
