    "q-72017": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72017",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(2,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(2,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(2,-1)=\\frac{2}{(-1)}=-2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-1) & =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\frac{0}{1}=0,\n$$\n\n  and  ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{-0}{{1}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
